course: dictionary
lesson: 15073
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can find the right one by reading the words around it. «The sailors climbed the rigging to set the sails.» Here, rigging means the ropes and wires on a ship.
explain: Look at the clues. Sailors, sails and climbing all point to a ship, so the meaning is about the ship's ropes.
explain: Now read «They were caught rigging the vote, which is against the law.» Rigging here means cheating, to fix the result in advance. The clues are vote and against the law.
explain: Try the meaning in the sentence. Does «putting up the ropes of a ship» make sense in a sentence about a vote? No, so you know it is a different meaning.
fact: Rigging is a noun for all the ropes, wires and chains that hold up a ship's masts and work the sails.
fact: «Rigging» can also mean cheating, to fix the result of something before it happens, as in «vote rigging».
fact: «Rigging» can mean putting up equipment, like rigging lights for a school show.
fact: Rig is a verb, so rigging is its -ing form: «The crew are rigging the lights.»
fact: When a word has more than one meaning, check the clues in the sentence to find the right one.
exercise: «The sailors climbed up the rigging to set the sails.» What is the rigging? | The big waves under the boat | The wooden deck of the ship | The anchor at the bottom of the sea | *The ropes and wires that hold up the masts and work the sails
exercise: «Rigging the vote is against the law.» What does rigging the vote mean? | *Cheating to fix the result in advance | Putting up posters for the vote | Counting every vote twice | Asking people to vote early
exercise: «The crew spent the afternoon rigging the lights for the school show.» What are they doing? | Sailing across the sea | Singing a song for the audience | *Putting up the lights and equipment | Cooking lunch for everyone
test: «The stage crew spent the afternoon rigging the lights.» Which meaning fits best here? | Cleaning the floor | *Putting up equipment | Climbing a mast | Cheating in a game
test: Which word means cheating to fix a result in advance? | sailing | *rigging | mast | deck
exam: «Why is rigging an election against the law?» | Because it uses too many ropes | *Because it cheats and fixes the result in advance | Because it is too loud | Because it makes people sleepy
